# BHK Connect for OpenCode

This guide covers two setup paths:

- **OpenCode v2 (2.0.30 or later):** Install the BHK Connect npm plugin for dynamic catalog discovery, Chat Completions and Responses support, and `/binnies-*` commands.
- **OpenCode v1:** Configure OpenCode's native OpenAI-compatible provider. This path uses a static model list and supports Chat Completions models only; the v2 plugin is not compatible with v1.

For both paths, use your BHK user virtual key, not the portal service key.

## OpenCode v2: Install from npm

After the package is published, add it to the `plugins` list in your OpenCode v2 `opencode.json` or `opencode.jsonc`. OpenCode resolves the package from npm; a separate global `npm install` is not required.

```json
{
  "$schema": "https://opencode.ai/config.json",
  "plugins": [
    {
      "package": "opencode-bhk-connect",
      "options": {
        "baseURL": "https://llm.binnieshkdigital.com/v1",
        "apiFormat": "auto"
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

`baseURL` defaults to `https://llm.binnieshkdigital.com/v1`. Remote endpoints must use HTTPS; HTTP is accepted only for localhost development. `apiFormat` can be `auto`, `responses`, or `chat_completions`. Catalog `api_types` are advisory, because a deployment can accept a format the catalog does not advertise: in `auto` the plugin tests both formats for each model after a key is connected and uses Responses when both work, otherwise the only working format, and an explicit `responses` or `chat_completions` choice is always accepted. This costs one small synthetic request per format and model, and results are cached until you run `/binnies-refresh` or change the key. If neither format works, the model keeps the format declared in the catalog and `/binnies-status` reports it.

Restart OpenCode, run `/connect`, select **BHK Connect**, and enter your BHK user virtual key. Then choose a model from `/models` under the `binnies` provider. Inference requests include `X-Usage-Tag: opencode`; catalog discovery requests do not.

## OpenCode v1: Native provider

OpenCode v1 does not support this v2 plugin API. Instead, configure a native custom provider in `opencode.json` or `opencode.jsonc` and define the models you want available:

```json
{
  "$schema": "https://opencode.ai/config.json",
  "provider": {
    "binnies": {
      "npm": "@ai-sdk/openai-compatible",
      "name": "BHK Connect",
      "options": {
        "baseURL": "https://llm.binnieshkdigital.com/v1"
      },
      "models": {
        "MODEL_ID_FROM_CATALOG": {
          "name": "MODEL_DISPLAY_NAME"
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

Replace the example model entry with IDs and display names from `GET https://llm.binnieshkdigital.com/v1/models`. Add only models whose catalog metadata declares `chat_completions` support and does not mark streaming as `false`. OpenCode resolves `@ai-sdk/openai-compatible` automatically; do not install it globally. Run `/connect`, select **BHK Connect**, and enter your BHK user virtual key.

The v1 model list is static and must be updated in the config when the catalog changes. This native-provider setup does not provide the v2 plugin's automatic discovery, Responses support, or `/binnies-*` commands.

## Set up with your coding agent

Copy this prompt into your coding agent while it is open in the project you want to configure:

```text
Set up BHK Connect for this OpenCode installation.

1. Detect whether the installed OpenCode is v1 or v2 (use `opencode --version` if available). Inspect the existing OpenCode config before editing it. Preserve all existing settings, comments, providers, and plugins; make only the necessary change and do not add duplicate entries.
2. For OpenCode v2 (2.0.30 or later), add this entry to the existing `plugins` array in `opencode.json` or `opencode.jsonc`:
   {
     "package": "opencode-bhk-connect",
     "options": {
       "baseURL": "https://llm.binnieshkdigital.com/v1",
       "apiFormat": "auto"
     }
   }
   Do not add a separate global npm install; OpenCode resolves the package from npm.
3. For OpenCode v1, do not add `opencode-bhk-connect` as a plugin. Configure a native provider with ID `binnies`, npm provider `@ai-sdk/openai-compatible`, display name `BHK Connect`, and base URL `https://llm.binnieshkdigital.com/v1`. Fetch `GET https://llm.binnieshkdigital.com/v1/models` if available and add only models whose metadata declares `chat_completions` support and does not mark streaming as false. Use each model ID as the config key and its catalog display name as the model name. If the catalog cannot be fetched, do not invent model IDs; ask me for the model IDs to add.
4. Do not ask me to paste a key into chat or put a key in the config, source files, or command history. After editing, tell me to run `/connect`, select BHK Connect, and enter my BHK user virtual key. Never use the portal service key.
5. Keep the existing config format (JSON or JSONC), validate the edited config if possible, and summarize the file and changes. Mention that v1 uses a static Chat Completions model list, while v2 provides dynamic discovery and `/binnies-*` commands.
```

## OpenCode v2 plugin commands

- `/binnies-refresh` forces a fresh catalog request, re-tests each model's API formats, and updates the provider's model list.
- `/binnies-status` reports the current discovery status, including models that accepted neither API format.
- `/binnies-format <model-id> <auto|responses|chat_completions>` sets a persistent, endpoint-specific protocol preference for one model. An explicit choice is accepted even when the catalog does not declare it and skips the capability test.

Reasoning variants are exposed only when declared by the catalog for the selected protocol. Models marked as non-streaming are omitted because this provider uses OpenCode's streaming chat transports.
